> [!answer]
> Because these are the body's **portals of entry — the tissues at the interface with the external environment**, and therefore the sites of **first and greatest exposure** to xenobiotics:
> - the **skin** meets chemicals by dermal contact;
> - the **gastrointestinal tract** meets everything in food, water and oral drugs;
> - the **upper respiratory tract** meets gases, vapours, dusts and smoke in inhaled air;
> - the **liver** receives the entire portal blood supply, so everything absorbed from the gut passes through it first.
>
> Siting a broad, inducible battery of phase I and phase II enzymes at these interfaces provides a **metabolic barrier (first-pass/presystemic metabolism)** that inactivates and prepares foreign chemicals for excretion **before they reach the systemic circulation** and the vulnerable internal organs. The enzymes are deliberately **non-specific and inducible** because the range of possible xenobiotics is unlimited.

> [!answer]
> Collect and analyse **urine** (ideally a **timed/24-hour collection**, with **paired blood samples**) and measure the drug and its metabolites by a specific analytical method — **immunoassay for screening, confirmed by HPLC, GC-MS or LC-MS/MS**.
>
> Practically:
> - take a **timed urine collection** so that the **amount excreted per unit time** (and renal clearance, $CL_R = \dfrac{U \times V}{P}$) can be calculated;
> - analyse **paired plasma and urine** samples to relate plasma concentration to urinary recovery and derive the pharmacokinetics;
> - **assess renal function** at the same time (serum creatinine, eGFR, urine output), because impaired excretion causes accumulation and toxicity;
> - note **urine pH and flow rate**, which alter the excretion of ionisable drugs (ion trapping).
